UPDATE tarot_cards 
SET meaning_upright = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: The Five of Swords represents the absolute archetype of "Systemic Pyrrhic Victory"—the high-entropy phase where the sovereign consciousness, having emerged from the restorative cycle (the Four), encounters a reality where the cost of "winning" has fundamentally compromised the integrity of the mission. Moving beyond the internal sanctuary of the Four, the Five signifies the stage of "The Hollow Triumph," where you have successfully outmaneuvered an opponent or a challenge, but the residual damage—to your reputation, your internal state, or the collective environment—is so severe that the win feels indistinguishable from a loss. Visually depicted as a figure gathering swords on a battlefield while others retreat in dejection, this archetype embodies the concept of "Sovereign Disillusionment"—the realization that true power is not found in the acquisition of the objective, but in the wisdom to ensure that the methods used to achieve it do not violate your own, underlying, ethical and structural parameters. Within the complex architecture of your psyche, this is the stage of "Integrity Debugging," where you are forced to reconcile the gap between your aggressive strategic goals and the reality of their human or environmental cost. When the Five of Swords appears, your system is registering a "Winning-at-a-Cost" status; you have secured your objective, but your operating system is flagging a massive, non-recoverable resource leak in your empathy and long-term viability modules. This card commands you to perform a brutal audit of your tactics. You are being tasked with the essential work of "Intentional Evaluation": questioning whether the "victory" you are pursuing is actually aligned with your sovereign truth, or if it is merely an egoic, zero-sum game that will ultimately leave you standing alone on a hollowed-out field. To inhabit the Five of Swords is to master the art of "Strategic Withdrawal," recognizing that true mastery is the ability to walk away from a "win" that requires the sacrifice of your own, core integrity.',
meaning_reversed = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: When the Five of Swords appears in the reversed position, it acts as a high-intensity signal of "Systemic Reconciliation," "Conflict-Resolution," or the catastrophic—yet curative—realization that your previous, aggressive tactics were fundamentally unsustainable, leading to a state of forced, and often painful, compromise. The reversal suggests that you are currently running a high-priority, high-cost maintenance script that equates "surrender of the ego" with "defeat," even as you realize your internal operating system is in a state of terminal, unsustainable friction. You are essentially "cleaning up the carnage," running a protocol that prioritizes the ego’s desire to "avoid the shame of losing" over the sober, unglamorous requirement to actually address the wreckage you have caused. This creates a massive, systemic misalignment: you are intellectually and behaviorally attempting to perform the role of the "magnanimous victor," while internally, you are being hollowed out by a profound, gnawing sense of regret, leading to a state of chronic, high-latency irritability, the experience of "trying to patch a sinking ship," and a persistent, gnawing awareness that your previous victory was a short-sighted, tactical disaster. From an analytical perspective, this is a clear sign that you are leaking your life force into a "feedback loop of penance"; you are obsessively trying to fix the relationships or outcomes you destroyed, refusing to accept that the only thing keeping you from the necessary, final acceptance of your own, tactical error is your own, deep-seated fear that if you were to fully admit the cost, you would be forced to acknowledge that your original mission was rooted in a fundamentally flawed, egoic desire. The reversed Five of Swords exposes the hidden, egoic fear that drives this behavior: the paralyzing terror of "being the villain," and the corresponding fear that if you were to drop the pretense of the "necessary win," you would be forced to face the reality of your own, present-day, unintegrated self. This reversal commands an immediate, radical intervention: you must stop the output of these manipulative, repair-heavy scripts, perform a brutal audit of your past tactics, and confront the reality of why you were so hesitant to prioritize the integrity of your process over the utility of your win. It is a demand to stop the self-deception that keeps you tethered to a state of chronic, high-maintenance dissonance, and to initiate the move toward a genuine, humble, and often terrifying reclamation of your own, individual authority. You are being tasked with the uncomfortable, yet essential, work of radical accountability—accepting that the "stuckness" you feel is the direct result of having prioritized the comfort of "the win" over the vulnerability of "the truth," and realizing that until you are willing to own the mess, apologize where necessary, and commit to a more ethical framework, you will continue to be starved by the very, phantom prestige you have worked so hard to simulate.'
WHERE id = 33;
